WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.636
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Jake Tapper.

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:05.504
As we mentioned, you're doing
your show at 9:00 p.m.

00:00:05.504 --> 00:00:06.972
through the midterms.

00:00:06.972 --> 00:00:08.374
-Yeah.
-This is going to be --

00:00:08.374 --> 00:00:10.342
You know, I think people
have said this a lot.

00:00:10.342 --> 00:00:11.911
This is the biggest midterm
in our lifetime.

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:13.213
This does seem like a big one,

00:00:13.213 --> 00:00:15.281
and I'm wondering
how you approach covering it

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:18.518
because it seems like
you have one party

00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:21.220
digging their heels in,
in advance,

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.355
about, "This election
might be stolen from us again."

00:00:23.355 --> 00:00:24.890
-Yeah. In fact, Kari Lake,

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.259
the Republican nominee
in Arizona,

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:28.795
has basically said
she's only going to accept

00:00:28.795 --> 00:00:30.430
the results if she wins.
-Yep.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:33.833
-Which is an interesting
guiding principle.

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:36.268
-A nice piece of honesty, yeah.

00:00:36.268 --> 00:00:37.737
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, it's weird.

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:40.206
I mean, there's a bunch
of election liars,

00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:42.909
election deniers who are
in competitive races

00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:45.511
and could very likely win,
Kari Lake among them.

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:48.380
In Pennsylvania and Michigan
and Arizona.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.884
And it's something
that I think people in the media

00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:52.618
just need to be honest
and up-front about.

00:00:52.618 --> 00:00:55.021
-What about how the returns
come in?

00:00:55.021 --> 00:00:57.256
Because I think --
You've obviously been through

00:00:57.256 --> 00:00:59.459
so many of these election
nights, and sometimes

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:03.096
certain places report early,
and, you know, all of a sudden,

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:05.698
you have a candidate
who is leading 80-20.

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:08.267
But people there,
people in the room know

00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:10.336
this isn't
a real number yet, right?

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:13.339
We have -- The bluer areas
of the state haven't reported.

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:15.608
Like, do you have
long discussions about

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.310
how you put up the results?

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.145
-Well, when the results
come in,

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.314
we try to make sure
people understand

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:22.615
these are early ballots

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.183
or this state
has these kinds of rules.

00:01:24.183 --> 00:01:26.018
You know,
it's very frustrating for me

00:01:26.018 --> 00:01:29.222
because I'm from Pennsylvania,
and the legislature there,

00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:33.292
which is Republican-led,
will not let the election boards

00:01:33.292 --> 00:01:35.461
not only count them
ahead of time,

00:01:35.461 --> 00:01:38.197
the votes that come in in early
voting or absentee ballots,

00:01:38.197 --> 00:01:39.632
but even prepare them,

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:41.434
meaning you can't even take them
out of the envelope.

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:45.604
So a whole bunch of votes
will not be counted that day

00:01:45.604 --> 00:01:47.273
just because
of how the rules are.

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:48.875
And yet people
will then complain

00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:50.576
about how inefficient
the system is

00:01:50.576 --> 00:01:52.412
as if there's some hijinks
going on.

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:54.914
And Trump took advantage of this
in 2020.

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:58.284
He made it very clear he was
going to declare victory early,

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:00.019
knowing that there were
still going to be

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:02.221
millions of votes to be counted.

00:02:02.221 --> 00:02:03.856
-I want to ask
what your take is --

00:02:03.856 --> 00:02:07.026
Obviously very early days of
Elon Musk in charge of Twitter.

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:12.031
But we've already seen a few
things where he's magnifying --

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:13.532
he's magnifying news stories

00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:17.736
that are sort of unsourced
and poorly reported.

00:02:17.736 --> 00:02:19.906
-They're not "unsourced
and poorly reported."

00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:21.240
They're nuts!
-Yeah.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.443
-They're crazy.
-Thank you.

00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:25.178
-He -- Okay.

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:26.545
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:26.545 --> 00:02:28.581
No, but it's --

00:02:28.581 --> 00:02:31.884
There's bad journalism,
and then there's crazy, right?

00:02:31.884 --> 00:02:34.087
-Right.
-I mean, this is crazy.

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:36.322
So, Hillary Clinton cited
something about the attack

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:39.425
on Nancy Pelosi's husband being
due to right-wing rhetoric,

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:41.193
and isn't that unfortunate,
et cetera?

00:02:41.193 --> 00:02:43.162
And Elon Musk wrote back to her
on Twitter,

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:44.764
"There might be
something more --

00:02:44.764 --> 00:02:46.365
a little bit more to the story."

00:02:46.365 --> 00:02:48.701
And linked to a website
that literally --

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:50.903
Well, that had a smear campaign
about Paul Pelosi,

00:02:50.903 --> 00:02:53.072
but, more importantly,
had declared

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:55.274
that Hillary Clinton died
in 2016...

00:02:55.274 --> 00:02:57.343
-Yeah.
-...and that a body double

00:02:57.343 --> 00:02:58.978
had actually debated
Donald Trump.

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:01.213
That was the --
That was the source.

00:03:01.213 --> 00:03:03.449
That's the source material
that we're dealing with here.

00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:05.751
-So it's actually good that
Elon said there might be more

00:03:05.751 --> 00:03:10.289
to the story than you think...
if you are who you say you are.

00:03:10.289 --> 00:03:13.559
-Right. Right.
"Hillary Clinton."

00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:15.294
-But I think -- Right.

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:17.863
That's the scary thing
about this early approach,

00:03:17.863 --> 00:03:20.833
is it does seem as though
he likes to throw things out

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:23.269
and say,
"Just worth mullin' over."

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:25.838
-Yeah.
-And that is not very helpful

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:28.908
as far as moving forward
the political discourse.

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:33.046
-Yeah, look, I'm generally
on the freer-speech debate,

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:36.482
that the solution to speech
you don't like is more speech.

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:39.085
But generally speaking,
the idea that not only

00:03:39.085 --> 00:03:42.355
was he allowing this
to be on the platform,

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:44.924
which you can make a decision
about that one way or the other,

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:46.759
that he was actually
pushing it out as a guy

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:49.262
with 110 million followers
on Twitter

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:52.231
is not cause for confidence

00:03:52.231 --> 00:03:54.133
about what the platform's
gonna be like in the future.

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:55.934
But we'll see.
It's early days yet, early days.

00:03:55.934 --> 00:03:58.204
-It is early days. I think
he's gonna turn it around.

00:03:58.204 --> 00:04:00.105
[ Laughter ]

00:04:00.105 --> 00:04:02.675
-You're not even on it
that much anymore.

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:04.343
-Look, I'm not going to pay

00:04:04.343 --> 00:04:05.878
20 bucks a month
for a blue checkmark.

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:07.380
Are you kidding me?

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.548
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:09.548 --> 00:04:12.818
Look. I am --
Let me explain why.

00:04:12.818 --> 00:04:15.488
I have a severe
gambling problem.

00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:17.089
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:18.891
I want to talk about this,
not to --

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:20.426
-Yeah.
-This is a very serious

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:23.996
and incredibly well-reported
piece of journalism

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:25.497
that you have
a family connection to.

00:04:25.497 --> 00:04:27.500
So, this is a story
about a young man

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:31.003
who was convicted for a crime

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:32.371
that he very likely
didn't commit.

00:04:32.371 --> 00:04:34.106
But the one thing you make clear
in the story

00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:36.108
is, whether you think
he committed the crime or not,

00:04:36.108 --> 00:04:39.211
no one would agree that he got
good legal representation.

00:04:39.211 --> 00:04:42.048
-He had a horrible lawyer.
My dad was his pediatrician.

00:04:42.048 --> 00:04:43.816
This is C.J.

00:04:43.816 --> 00:04:45.651
C.J. Rice was 17.

00:04:45.651 --> 00:04:48.688
C.J. had been shot, and my dad

00:04:48.688 --> 00:04:52.025
saw him in his pediatric office
in South Philadelphia,

00:04:52.025 --> 00:04:53.625
and C.J. could barely walk.

00:04:53.625 --> 00:04:55.961
Five days later,
there was another shooting,

00:04:55.961 --> 00:04:59.265
a horrible thing,
and I think it's kind of shady,

00:04:59.265 --> 00:05:02.502
but ultimately somebody said
that C.J. did it,

00:05:02.502 --> 00:05:05.904
even though the shooters
had been seen running.

00:05:05.904 --> 00:05:07.907
And C.J., my dad, other people
who knew him thought,

00:05:07.907 --> 00:05:10.109
"Well, there's no way they're
going to convict him of this

00:05:10.109 --> 00:05:11.244
or even charge him for it.

00:05:11.244 --> 00:05:12.578
He can barely walk,
much less run."

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:14.213
-He turned himself in.
-He turned himself in.

00:05:14.213 --> 00:05:16.048
-Because he was so confident
that based on the fact

00:05:16.048 --> 00:05:18.250
that he could not run,
this would be a situation

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:20.920
where they would just let him
walk out of the police station.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.522
-Right, and his mom worked
for the District Attorney's
office, et cetera.

00:05:23.522 --> 00:05:24.790
He gave them his phone,
thinking,

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:26.992
"Well, they're clearly
going to check to see

00:05:26.992 --> 00:05:28.861
that my phone was actually
in West Philadelphia

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:31.063
that day, not South Philadelphia
where the shooting was."

00:05:31.063 --> 00:05:33.132
But none of that happened,
and instead he didn't get a --

00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:34.900
He wasn't lucky enough
to get a public defender.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:37.236
He's poor, so he couldn't afford
a good attorney

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:39.472
that you or I would have
been able to afford.

00:05:39.472 --> 00:05:41.007
He didn't get a public defender,

00:05:41.007 --> 00:05:43.276
which are generally pretty good
lawyers, public defenders.

00:05:43.276 --> 00:05:44.710
He had a court-appointed
attorney,

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.211
and some of them are great.

00:05:46.211 --> 00:05:48.347
But the records, statistically
and according to studies,

00:05:48.347 --> 00:05:51.250
is much worse, and she was just
a horrible lawyer, horrible.

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:56.121
And one instance after another,
she failed him,

00:05:56.121 --> 00:05:58.290
making -- you know,
having it be in juvenile court

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.726
because he was 17,
going to the crime scene,

00:06:00.726 --> 00:06:02.461
challenging
the one piece of evidence

00:06:02.461 --> 00:06:04.330
against him over and over.

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:06.332
And going through this case,
which my dad asked me to

00:06:06.332 --> 00:06:10.603
look into after years of trying
to get him out of prison,

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:12.604
it really just opened
my eyes about how --

00:06:12.604 --> 00:06:14.273
I mean, we know it
intellectually,

00:06:14.273 --> 00:06:16.275
but there really are just
two systems of justice,

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:17.743
one for people
who can afford it --

00:06:17.743 --> 00:06:19.578
I would never have even been
charged with this crime,

00:06:19.578 --> 00:06:21.647
and if I had been,
I would have gotten a lawyer

00:06:21.647 --> 00:06:23.348
who would have gotten it
thrown out of court.

00:06:23.348 --> 00:06:26.052
But this kid, you know,
he got the bad end of the stick

00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:27.987
when it comes
to this sort of thing.

00:06:27.987 --> 00:06:29.922
And he couldn't afford
a good lawyer,

00:06:29.922 --> 00:06:31.424
and he had a horrible one.

00:06:31.424 --> 00:06:35.661
And now he's just in prison
for 30 to 60 years for a crime

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:38.664
in which nobody
was even seriously wounded,

00:06:38.664 --> 00:06:40.800
much less killed.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.701
And it's just --
it's not justice.

00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:47.272
And so, you know, I hear a lot
about crime on TV ads,

00:06:47.272 --> 00:06:50.009
and, you know, I'll be doing
a piece about it on my show.

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:51.443
But, you know, the idea

00:06:51.443 --> 00:06:54.080
that anytime anybody challenges
a conviction

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:56.782
that they're "soft on crime" --
I'm not soft on crime.

00:06:56.782 --> 00:06:59.317
I want anybody guilty
of a shooting offense

00:06:59.317 --> 00:07:01.187
in jail -- in prison.

00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:02.755
But I don't think he did it,

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:04.323
and that means
the people who did do it,

00:07:04.323 --> 00:07:05.824
they're still out there.

00:07:05.824 --> 00:07:07.993
-Well, it's a really wonderful
piece of reporting.

00:07:07.993 --> 00:07:10.395
The connection to your father
and the role he played,

00:07:10.395 --> 00:07:14.100
the really noble role he played,
it should be noted, as well,

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.034
makes for great reading.
Thank you for being here.

00:07:16.034 --> 00:07:17.403
It's always a pleasure
to see you.

00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:19.238
-So good to see you in person.
Yeah, thank you.

00:07:19.238 --> 00:07:20.639
-You guys, that's Jake Tapper.

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:22.742
"CNN Tonight with Jake Tapper"

00:07:22.742 --> 00:07:24.543
airs nightly on CNN
through the midterms,

00:07:24.543 --> 00:07:26.646
and his cover story
for "The Atlantic" is out now.

00:07:26.646 --> 00:07:28.714
We'll be right back
with Sheryl Lee Ralph.

